# Grammar — examples & insert options always in view

When a grammar page has **many numbered items** (dialogues, transformations, gap fills) plus **patterns / word box / example (0)**:

## Hard UX

1. **Two columns on desktop:** tasks scroll on the left; a **sticky rail** on the right holds what the learner must keep seeing.
2. Rail contents (as relevant to the book):
   - short **pattern reminders** (e.g. `used to + V1` / `be used to + V-ing` / `get used to + V-ing`);
   - **Example (0)** or model line — not only buried at the top of a long list;
   - **chips / word box** for forms or particles the learner inserts.
3. Learner scrolls **down through items 1 → N** and still sees patterns + insert options **without scrolling back up**.
4. Prefer **click-to-insert** chips into the focused gap (optional but good).
5. Follow `no-unnecessary-scroll-sticky-rails.mdc`: rail should **fit** on a typical laptop without its own scrollbar if possible; parent grid `align-items: stretch`.

## Anti-patterns

- Example (0) + word box only **above** a long list — disappears when you work on item 6–8.
- Making the learner scroll the whole page up every time to re-read the pattern.
- Hiding the insert bank inside a collapsed `<details>` by default.

## Etalon

`Grammar/unit1-used-to/dialogues.html` — sticky rail: patterns + example (0) + insert chips beside dialogue gaps.

